WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:04.170
For the most part, it’s pretty good being
alive.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:09.490
There are tacos, videos of monkeys, cheap
air travel and many exciting hats to choose

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:10.490
from.

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:15.289
But it all flashes by very quickly and you
barely get a moment to enjoy it between; sleeping,

00:00:15.289 --> 00:00:19.960
school, work, waiting for Windows 10 to install,
and torrenting Star Wars.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.190
And before you know it, it’s all over and
you’re floating away from your body wondering

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:28.540
what you could have achieved if you’d skipped
season 6 of How I Met Your Mother and gone

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.790
out to sit in the sunshine for a while.

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:33.140
So how do we hold on longer?

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:37.379
Can we avoid the inevitable end because we
are just not ready yet?

00:00:37.379 --> 00:00:42.260
I mean come on, I’ve got tickets to Cirque
Du Soleil next week and I just bought a 3

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:44.329
for 1 toothpaste deal.

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:46.570
Can humans delay death?

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:52.499
Because, in the words of the great Terry Pratchett,
“inside every old person is a young person

00:00:52.499 --> 00:00:55.179
wondering what happened.”

00:00:55.179 --> 00:01:02.979
If you switch on the news for a few minutes,
you might feel like there is death and destruction

00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:06.710
lurking around every corner and that you’ll
be lucky to make it to lunch time.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:10.619
But the truth is, overall, we are living longer
than ever.

00:01:10.619 --> 00:01:17.210
If you look back to 200 years ago, life expectancy
in almost every country except the UK was

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:22.100
below 40 and as low as 25 in some of Africa
and Asia.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.170
Since then it has increased with strange regularity.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:30.100
On average, we have gained one year of lifespan
for every five years that have passed.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:34.730
There were obviously some dips after the world
wars, as well as the Spanish flu epidemic

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:41.009
in the early 20th century, but we have arrived
today at a global average of 71 years.

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:46.130
Women outlive men by about 4 to 5 years but
we’re still not sure why this is.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:50.770
If it was down to less manual work, you’d
have expected there to be less of a difference

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:55.390
now that we all sit on bean bags, staring
into spreadsheets for 8 hours a day, but it’s

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:56.390
unchanged.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:02:02.329
And if it’s the extra cigarettes, beer and
chicken wings men supposedly consume, then

00:02:02.329 --> 00:02:05.409
why does the same difference occur in apes?

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:12.040
A Korean scientist named Han-Nam Park has
suggested it could be the effects of testosterone,

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:17.190
citing a study that showed eunuchs, that’s
men who have had their testicles removed,

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:20.160
lived noticeably longer than other men.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.160
Um…

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:24.180
I think I’m fine with the five-year difference
actually.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:29.069
It’s diet and medicine that keep pushing
our life expectancy up.

00:02:29.069 --> 00:02:33.580
Although there must be some upper limit to
how long a human can live, there have been

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.980
no signs that we have reached it yet.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:41.299
We will eventually arrive at a 100-year average
and probably keep going.

00:02:41.299 --> 00:02:44.599
Soon we’re going to get pretty bored of
the sight of each other.

00:02:44.599 --> 00:02:50.050
Heart disease and strokes are by far our biggest
killers but as the treatments and care for

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:53.360
these improve, we are getting better at fighting
them.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.970
Both are linked to high blood pressure and
high cholesterol, so doctors can help patients

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:02.590
with a range of lifestyle changes and medications
to lower their risk.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:07.580
Just 100 years ago, these killers were a lot
less common, not because people were leading

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:13.879
healthier lives; far from it, statistics show
we actually eat healthier now, even with the

00:03:13.879 --> 00:03:15.310
prevalence of fast food.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:21.180
But it was that people were dying much earlier
from infectious diseases such as pneumonia,

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:22.409
flu and tuberculosis.

00:03:22.409 --> 00:03:27.000
Now that’s what happens when your national
cures are mainly whiskey, cigarettes, sea

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.590
air and radium.

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:34.060
They weren’t glowing with health, they were
just pumped full of radiation.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:40.010
Aside from combatting disease, we have spent
many years looking into slowing the aging

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:41.280
process itself.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:47.599
We’re not clear on exactly why we age but
a fairly common theory is that over time your

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:53.739
DNA loses its ability to withstand damage
and to repair itself, making mutations more

00:03:53.739 --> 00:03:54.829
likely.

00:03:54.829 --> 00:03:58.950
And these aren’t the cool mutations that
give you laser eyes and teleportation, these

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:03.049
are just your cells ceasing to function in
the way you need them to.

00:04:03.049 --> 00:04:09.200
Your body renews almost perfectly between
the ages of about 20 to 35, and after that

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.719
your cells begin to lose interest and start
thinking about retiring, maybe to go sit by

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:16.519
a nice beach somewhere down by your kidneys.

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:21.870
So one way to combat this is to poke around
in the DNA and find the troublemakers.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:30.330
The Buck Institute 238 genes that could be
deleted and increase the lifespan of yeast.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:36.650
And you might be thinking; Oh yeast, whoopty-doo,
I always wanted immortal bread, but your DNA

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:40.960
shares much more in common with a slice of
toast than you might think.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:45.980
That’s right, when you eat your lovely buttery
toast in the morning, you’re chomping down

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:49.310
on your DNA cousin, you monster!

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:55.010
They managed to get a massive 60% increase
in the life of these yeast strains and are

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:59.300
now looking to test this out a little higher
up the evolutionary chain.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:04.860
The key gene for this 60% jump is called LOS1,
which is linked to a master switch that is

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:07.160
associated with calorie restriction.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:13.070
It’s long been thought that calorie restriction
has been linked with longer lives.

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:18.120
After all, humans aren’t designed to gorge
ourselves every day on burgers and ever so

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:24.889
deliciously flaky, creamy, melt-in-your-mouth
pastries, yes, I know it’s difficult.

00:05:24.889 --> 00:05:30.130
But humans are hunter gatherers at heart,
our body has evolved to go days without eating

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:33.889
at all, which was common for our prehistoric
ancestors.

00:05:33.889 --> 00:05:40.410
Interestingly, during the Irish Potato Famine,
national life expectancy actually rose from

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:44.490
40 years to 50, going against every expectation.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:49.690
Now, before you spit out your ramen and throw
away your banana, you’re not supposed to

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:50.800
starve yourself.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:53.720
Malnutrition has a terrible impact on your
body.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:59.500
The key is to walk that fine line of getting
just the calories you need to survive.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:04.280
The research is still a little vague, partly
because no matter how dedicated you are, it’s

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:08.319
hard to get someone to have that much self-control
for 10 years or so.

00:06:08.319 --> 00:06:12.660
I mean, I’ve only just mentioned food and
you’re already thinking about what’s in

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:16.990
your fridge; yes, that tub of Ben &amp; Jerry’s
will still be there when this video’s finished,

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:18.599
please sit back down.

00:06:18.599 --> 00:06:24.220
Experiments on mice that involve just limiting
their food on alternative days, known as intermittent

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:30.560
fasting, has resulted in longer living mice
and a reduction in age-related diseases such

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:33.630
as Alzheimer's, diabetes and cancer.

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:36.620
All from just missing a few meals each week.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:38.680
It could work for a few reasons.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:43.080
The reduced energy in your body may mean your
core temperature is slightly lower so your

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.650
general metabolism happens a little slower
too.

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:51.910
Also, less cellular division means there is
less chance for your DNA to become damaged.

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:56.669
So if you don’t want to eat less, maybe
just reduce your core heat by sitting in a

00:06:56.669 --> 00:06:58.539
bath full of frozen peas and ice cream.

00:06:58.539 --> 00:07:00.430
I should be a doctor.

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:10.370
The metabolism is clearly a big part of the
aging issue.

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:16.360
Researchers at Cardiff University were investigating
type 2 diabetes and they noticed that patients

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:23.300
who took the drug metformin lived, on average,
15 percent longer than a group of healthy

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:29.129
people who didn’t have diabetes and weren’t
taking metformin but were similar in nearly

00:07:29.129 --> 00:07:34.099
all other respects, in terms of age, lifestyle,
favourite Game of Thrones character and so

00:07:34.099 --> 00:07:35.099
on.

00:07:35.099 --> 00:07:39.740
This is very surprising because diabetes,
even when controlled, should shorten your

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:40.750
lifespan.

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:45.789
It could be because metformin interferes with
a process called glycation.

00:07:45.789 --> 00:07:50.669
This is a major metabolic process where sugars
attach to proteins but it leaves behind a

00:07:50.669 --> 00:07:56.460
thing called Advanced Glycation Endproducts,
or AGEs ironically.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:01.230
This is bad and they basically gum up your
biomolecular workings like when you got cake

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:02.930
in your keyboard.

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:09.370
Because we are a greedy and ridiculous species,
we’ve actual been adding AGEs to our food,

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:15.710
typically things with caramelization like
donuts, barbequed meat and dark sugared drinks.

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:17.970
Why does evil have to be so delicious?

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:22.060
Our last way of living longer sounds like
it’s been ripped from the pages of some

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:24.050
creepy Victorian novel.

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:30.419
In 2005, Thomas Rando from Stanford University,
found that he could rejuvenate older mice

00:08:30.419 --> 00:08:34.690
by surgically linking their blood flow to
younger mice.

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:39.250
It apparently works best if there is a new
moon, the young mouse is a virgin and stays

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:41.210
off garlic-flavoured mouse food.

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:45.060
But maybe I’ve been reading too many cheesy
vampire novels.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:50.260
This vampiric practise enabled the stem cells
of doddering old rodents to suddenly become

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:56.510
effective again and begin to repair damaged
cells, build new tissue and stop them being

00:08:56.510 --> 00:08:58.970
so confused about smart phones.

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:05.090
Harvard University biologist Amy Wagers looked
into this further and believes it is a protein

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:11.140
called GDF11 that is responsible and that
this is what is lost in older mice.

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:16.890
She managed to get similar results to Rando
by just injecting them with the protein, but

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:22.120
Rando said that his creepy vampire way was
much more fun and gave him an excuse to wear

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:26.190
a cape and play My Chemical Romance at full
volume all day.

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:31.560
However, because this is science, recently
a team from Cambridge, Massachusetts claimed

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:37.380
that GDF11 actually increases with age and
acts to inhibit muscle generation.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:39.500
So, the complete opposite.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:44.400
See, this is why everyone hates you science,
stop contradicting yourself!

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.590
But before you start worrying about adding
a few years to the end of your days, you’re

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:51.170
probably better off dealing with the life
you have now.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:56.160
So, go ask out that boy or girl you like,
find a good book to read and if you’re worried

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.420
that eating that box of donuts will shave
a week off your existence, why don’t you

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:21.460
compromise by leaving that bit in the middle,
that’s where all the calories are 

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:29.690
you know.

